A(L[C]) . Proof. Since for any ( F , i ) ∈ S (F,i)\in S there exists c ∈ C c\in C such that { ( c , 0 ) , ( c , 1 ) } ⊆ ( F , i ) \{(c,0),(c,1)\}\subseteq(F,i) , and no maximal chain of L ⁡ [ C ] L[C] outside of S S contains such elements, we know that no edges from a vertex outside S S is pointing to ( F , i ) (F,i) in F ​ A ​ ( L ⁡ [ C ] ) FA(L[C]) . This proves that S S is a source set of F ​ A ​ ( L ⁡ [ C ] ) FA(L[C]) . Seeking a contradiction, suppose that there exist ( F , i ) ∉ T (F,i)\not\in T and ( G , 0 ) ∈ T (G,0)\in T such that ( F , i ) → ( G , 0 ) (F,i)\rightarrow(G,0) . Since ( F , i ) ∉ T (F,i)\not\in T , we have F ∩ C ≠ ∅ F\cap C\neq\emptyset , meaning i ≠ 0 i\neq 0 . We have | ( G , 0 ) | = | G | |(G,0)|=|G| and | ( F , i ) | = | F | + 1 |(F,i)|=|F|+1 since i ≠ 0 i\neq 0 . In general, if F → F ′ F\rightarrow F^{\prime} in F ​ A ​ ( L ) FA(L) , it follows that | F | ≥ | F ′ | |F|\geq|F^{\prime}| . Then ( F , i ) → ( G , 0 ) ⟹ | F | + 1 ≥ | G | (F,i)\rightarrow(G,0)\Longrightarrow|F|+1\geq|G| . Since C C does not intersect the spine of L L , by Lemma 2.16 we have ℓ ⁡ ( L ⁡ [ C ] ) = ℓ ⁡ ( L ) \ell(L[C])=\ell(L) . Thus | G | ≤ | F | + 1 = | ( F , i ) | ≤ ℓ ⁡ ( L ⁡ [ C ] ) = ℓ ⁡ ( L ) = | G | |G|\leq|F|+1=|(F,i)|\leq\ell(L[C])=\ell(L)=|G| . Then | ( F , i ) | = | G | = ℓ ⁡ ( L ⁡ [ C ] ) |(F,i)|=|G|=\ell(L[C]) , thus ( F , i ) (F,i) is a longest chain of L ⁡ [ C ] L[C] . Since ( F , i ) ∉ T (F,i)\not\in T , which means F F is not a longest chain of L L , it implies that ( F , i ) ∈ S (F,i)\in S since the chains of longest length of L ⁡ [ C ] L[C] are either longest chains of L L or go through the doubling of C C . Then, as observed in the first part of the proof, ( F , i ) (F,i) has at least two elements ( c , 0 ) (c,0) and ( c , 1 ) (c,1) not in ( G , 0 ) (G,0) , which with | ( F , i ) | = | ( G , 0 ) | |(F,i)|=|(G,0)| makes it absurd to have | ( F , i ) ∩ ( G , 0 ) | = | ( G , 0 ) | − 1 |(F,i)\,\cap(G,0)|=|(G,0)|-1 , which is implied by ( F , i ) → ( G , 0 ) (F,i)\rightarrow(G,0) . It follows that T T is a source set. With S ∩ T = ∅ S\,\cap T=\emptyset since C C does not intersect the spine of L L , this finishes the proof. ∎ Now we can prove Theorem 3.24 : Proof of Theorem 3.24 . Let L = E ⁡ [ C 1 , … , C n ] L=E[C_{1},\dots,C_{n}] be a congruence normal lattice. Suppose that there exists i i such that C i C_{i} does not intersect the spine of E ⁡ [ C 1 , … , C i − 1 ] E[C_{1},\dots,C_{i-1}] . Then by Lemma 3.33 the graph F ​ A ​ ( E ⁡ [ C 1 , … , C i ] ) FA(E[C_{1},\dots,C_{i}]) has at least two disjoint source sets. By Lemma 3.32 , since L = E ⁡ [ C 1 , … , C n ] L=E[C_{1},\dots,C_{n}] is obtained from E ⁡ [ C 1 , … , C i ] E[C_{1},\dots,C_{i}] by doublings, we have that L L has also at least two disjoint source sets, which by Lemma 3.30 implies that L L is not shellable. ∎ 3.4. Induced subgraphs of a canonical join graph In this section we give, using the fundamental theorem of finite semidistributive lattices [ 47 ] , a counterexample to the following: Question 3.34 ( [ 6 , Question 2.5.5] ) . Is an induced subcomplex of a canonical join complex still a canonical join complex? As explained in Section 2 , for us a canonical join complex is always one coming from a semidistributive lattice (not just a join-semidistributive lattice). Question 3.34 was asked in this context. The canonical join complex being the clique complex of its 1-skeleton, which is the canonical join graph, this question is equivalent to asking whether an induced subgraph of a canonical join graph is still such a graph. Indeed, an induced subcomplex on vertices X X of a flag simplicial complex Δ \Delta is still flag, and its 1 1 -skeleton is the induced subgraph of the 1 1 -skeleton of Δ \Delta on vertices X X . v 0 v_{0} v 1 v_{1} v 2 v_{2} v 3 v_{3} v 4 v_{4} u 0 u_{0} u 1 u_{1} u 2 u_{2} u 3 u_{3} u 4 u_{4} w w t t Figure 5 . The Galois graph of a semidistributive lattice having 167 167 elements. The arrows are of the three types → \rig