p@{_scopebegin=1} { {}{{}}{} {}{}\lxSVG@begingroup@{_scopebegin=1} \lxSVG@setlinewidth{\the\pgflinewidth}\lxSVG@begingroup@{stroke-width=0.2pt} \lx@inpgf@ignorespaces{}{}{}{}{{}}{}{}{}{{}}\lxSVG@stroke\lxSVG@drawpath@unclipped{M 2.42 0 L 15.47 0}{fill:none} {{}{{}}{}{}{{}}{{{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}}{{{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}}{\lxSVG@begingroup@{_scopebegin=1} \lxSVG@transformcm{-1.0}{0.0}{0.0}{-1.0}{1.74994pt}{0.0pt}\lxSVG@begingroup@{transform=matrix(-1.0 0.0 0.0 -1.0 2.42 0)} \lxSVG@begingroup@{_scopebegin=1} \lxSVG@begingroup@{stroke-dasharray=none,stroke-dashoffset=0.0pt} \lxSVG@begingroup@{stroke-linecap=round} \lxSVG@drawpath@unclipped{M 0 0 L 0.46 0 C 1.47 0 2.28 -1.01 2.28 -1.83 C 2.28 -2.84 1.47 -3.65 0.46 -3.65}{fill:none} \lxSVG@closescope \lxSVG@closescope }}{{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}}}}{{}{{}}{}{}{{}}{{{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}}{{{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}}{\lxSVG@begingroup@{_scopebegin=1} \lxSVG@transformcm{1.0}{0.0}{0.0}{1.0}{11.28093pt}{0.0pt}\lxSVG@begingroup@{transform=matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 15.61 0)} \lxSVG@begingroup@{_scopebegin=1} \lxSVG@begingroup@{stroke-dasharray=none,stroke-dashoffset=0.0pt} \lxSVG@begingroup@{stroke-linecap=round} \lxSVG@begingroup@{stroke-linejoin=round} \lxSVG@drawpath@unclipped{M -2.55 2.82 C -2.08 1.13 -1.04 0.33 0 0 C -1.04 -0.33 -2.08 -1.13 -2.55 -2.82}{fill:none} \lxSVG@closescope \lxSVG@closescope }}{{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}}}}\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces \lxSVG@closescope } \lxSVG@closescope {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}\hss}\lxSVG@discardpath\lxSVG@closescope \hss}}\lxSVG@closescope\endpgfpicture}}}v_{1} . Let H := G ⁡ ( L ) ¯ H:=\overline{G(L)} . By Proposition 3.37 , H H is a canonical join graph. For a graph G = ( V , E ) G=(V,E) and X ⊆ V X\subseteq V , we denote by G ⁡ [ X ] G[X] the induced subgraph of G G on the elements of X X . Let X := V ⁡ ( H ) ∖ { t } X:=V(H)\setminus\{t\} , where V ⁡ ( H ) V(H) is the vertex-set of H H . Our goal is now to prove that H ⁡ [ X ] H[X] is not a canonical join graph. Thus it will be a counterexample to Question 3.34 . The following result is easy: Lemma 3.38 . Let G = ( V , E ) G=(V,E) be a multigraph and X ⊆ V X\subseteq V . We have G ⁡ [ X ] ¯ = G ¯ ​ [ X ] \overline{G[X]}=\overline{G}[X] . By definition, H ¯ = G ⁡ ( L ) \overline{H}=G(L) . Thus by Lemma 3.38 , we have H ⁡ [ X ] ¯ = H ¯ ​ [ X ] = G ​ ( L ) ​ [ X ] \overline{H[X]}=\overline{H}[X]=G(L)[X] . By Proposition 3.37 , H ⁡ [ X ] H[X] is a canonical join graph if and only if the underlying undirected graph of G ​ ( L ) ​ [ X ] G(L)[X] , or any multigraph obtained from it by adding some edges between two elements of G ​ ( L ) ​ [ X ] G(L)[X] already joined by an edge, is a two-acyclic factorization system. The underlying undirected graph of G ​ ( L ) ​ [ X ] G(L)[X] is called the Grötzsch graph ; it is a triangle-free graph of chromatic number 4 4 . These two properties will play an important role. Lemma 3.39 . Let G G be a simple graph. There exists an orientation of the edges of G G such that the resulting directed graph has no directed walk of length 3 3 if and only if χ ⁡ ( G ) ≤ 3 \chi(G)\leq 3 . Proof. Suppose that χ ⁡ ( G ) ≤ 3 \chi(G)\leq 3 . Let us choose a proper coloring C : V → { 0 , 1 , 2 } C:V\rightarrow\{0,1,2\} of G G . We deduce an orientation of the edges of G G by orienting an edge x → y x\rightarrow y if C ⁡ ( x ) < C ⁡ ( y ) C(x)<C(y) . This is an orientation of G G without directed walks of length 3 3 . Conversely, suppose that there exists an orientation of the edges of G G such that the resulting directed graph ( G , → ) (G,\rightarrow) has no directed walk of length 3 3 . Then ( G , → ) (G,\rightarrow) does not have cycles. This proves that the following coloring is well-defined; color any vertex x x with the longest length of a directed walk ending at x x . This gives a coloring C : V → { 0 , 1 , 2 } C:V\rightarrow\{0,1,2\} , which is proper since for each x → y x\rightarrow y , we have C ⁡ ( x ) < C ⁡ ( y ) C(x)<C(y) . ∎ Lemma 3.40 . A triangle-free simple directed graph G G forms a two-acyclic factorization system if and only if G G does not have a directed walk of length 3 3 . Proof. Since G G is simple, conditions ( i ) (i) and ( i ​ i ) (ii) of Definition 3.36 are satisfied. On the contrary, condition ( i ​ i ​ i ) (iii) cannot be satisfied if there are any → \rightarrow arrows since G G is triangle-free. Thus G G forms a two-acyclic factorization system if and only if G A G_{A} does not have regular arrows → \rightarrow . By the definitions of \mathrel{\hbox to11.58pt{\vbox to4.28pt{\pgfpicture\makeatletter\hbox{\hskip 0.09999pt\lower-2.13869pt\hbox to0.0pt{\lxSVG@begingroup@{_scopebegin=1} \lxSVG@begingroup@{stroke=#000000} \lxSVG@begingroup@{fill=#000000} \lxSVG@setlinewidth{\the\pgflinewidth}\lxSVG@begingroup@{stroke-width=0.4pt} \lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\nullfont\hbox to0.0pt{\lxSVG@begingroup@{_scopebegin=1} { {}{{}}{} {}{}\lxSVG@begingroup@{_scopebegin=1} \lxSVG@setlinewidth{\the\pgflinewidth}\lxSVG@begingroup@{stroke-width=0.2pt} \lx@inpgf@ignorespaces{}{}{}{}{{}}{}{}{}{{}}\lxSVG@stroke\lxSVG@drawpath@unclipped{M 2.42 0 L 15.47 0}{fill:none} {{}{{}}{}{}{{}}{{{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}}{{{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}}{\lxSVG@begingroup@{_scopebegin=1} \lxSVG@transformcm{-1.0}{0.0}{0.0}{-1.0}{1.74994pt}{0.0pt}\lxSVG@begingroup@{transform=matrix(-1.0 0.0 0.0 -1.0 2.42 0)} \lxSVG@begingroup@{_scopebegin=1} \lxSVG@begingroup@{stroke-dasharray=none,stroke-dashoffset=0.0pt} \lxSVG@begingroup@{stroke-linecap=round} \lxSVG@drawpath@unclipped{M 0 0 L 0.46 0 C 1.47 0 2.28 -1.01 2.28 -1.83 C 2.28 -2.84 1.47 -3.65 0.46 -3.65}{fill:none} \lxSVG@closescope \lxSVG@closescope }}{{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}}}}{{}{{}}{}{}{{}}{{{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}}{{{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}}{\lxSVG@begingroup@{_scopebegin=1} \lxSVG@transformcm{1.0}{0.0}{0.0}{1.0}{11.28093pt}{0.0pt}\lxSVG@begingroup@{transform=matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 15.61 0)} \lxSVG@begingroup@{_scopebegin=1} \lxSVG@begingroup@{stroke-dasharr