 By definitions of \mathrel{\hbox to11.58pt{\vbox to4.28pt{\pgfpicture\makeatletter\hbox{\hskip 0.09999pt\lower-2.13869pt\hbox to0.0pt{\lxSVG@begingroup@{_scopebegin=1} \lxSVG@begingroup@{stroke=#000000} \lxSVG@begingroup@{fill=#000000} \lxSVG@setlinewidth{\the\pgflinewidth}\lxSVG@begingroup@{stroke-width=0.4pt} \lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\nullfont\hbox to0.0pt{\lxSVG@begingroup@{_scopebegin=1} { {}{{}}{} {}{}\lxSVG@begingroup@{_scopebegin=1} \lxSVG@setlinewidth{\the\pgflinewidth}\lxSVG@begingroup@{stroke-width=0.2pt} \lx@inpgf@ignorespaces{}{}{}{}{{}}{}{}{}{{}}\lxSVG@stroke\lxSVG@drawpath@unclipped{M 2.42 0 L 15.47 0}{fill:none} {{}{{}}{}{}{{}}{{{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}}{{{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}}{\lxSVG@begingroup@{_scopebegin=1} \lxSVG@transformcm{-1.0}{0.0}{0.0}{-1.0}{1.74994pt}{0.0pt}\lxSVG@begingroup@{transform=matrix(-1.0 0.0 0.0 -1.0 2.42 0)} \lxSVG@begingroup@{_scopebegin=1} \lxSVG@begingroup@{stroke-dasharray=none,stroke-dashoffset=0.0pt} \lxSVG@begingroup@{stroke-linecap=round} \lxSVG@drawpath@unclipped{M 0 0 L 0.46 0 C 1.47 0 2.28 -1.01 2.28 -1.83 C 2.28 -2.84 1.47 -3.65 0.46 -3.65}{fill:none} \lxSVG@closescope \lxSVG@closescope }}{{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}}}}{{}{{}}{}{}{{}}{{{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}}{{{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}}{\lxSVG@begingroup@{_scopebegin=1} \lxSVG@transformcm{1.0}{0.0}{0.0}{1.0}{11.28093pt}{0.0pt}\lxSVG@begingroup@{transform=matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 15.61 0)} \lxSVG@begingroup@{_scopebegin=1} \lxSVG@begingroup@{stroke-dasharray=none,stroke-dashoffset=0.0pt} \lxSVG@begingroup@{stroke-linecap=round} \lxSVG@begingroup@{stroke-linejoin=round} \lxSVG@drawpath@unclipped{M -2.55 2.82 C -2.08 1.13 -1.04 0.33 0 0 C -1.04 -0.33 -2.08 -1.13 -2.55 -2.82}{fill:none} \lxSVG@closescope \lxSVG@closescope }}{{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}}}}\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces \lxSVG@closescope } \lxSVG@closescope {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}\hss}\lxSVG@discardpath\lxSVG@closescope \hss}}\lxSVG@closescope\endpgfpicture}}} and \mathrel{\hbox to11.58pt{\vbox to4.28pt{\pgfpicture\makeatletter\hbox{\hskip 0.09999pt\lower-2.13869pt\hbox to0.0pt{\lxSVG@begingroup@{_scopebegin=1} \lxSVG@begingroup@{stroke=#000000} \lxSVG@begingroup@{fill=#000000} \lxSVG@setlinewidth{\the\pgflinewidth}\lxSVG@begingroup@{stroke-width=0.4pt} \lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\nullfont\hbox to0.0pt{\lxSVG@begingroup@{_scopebegin=1} { {}{{}}{} {}{}\lxSVG@begingroup@{_scopebegin=1} \lxSVG@setlinewidth{\the\pgflinewidth}\lxSVG@begingroup@{stroke-width=0.2pt} \lx@inpgf@ignorespaces{}{}{}{}{}{{}}\lxSVG@stroke\lxSVG@drawpath@unclipped{M 0 0 L 15.47 0}{fill:none} {{}{{}}{}{}{{}}{{{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}}{{{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}}{\lxSVG@begingroup@{_scopebegin=1} \lxSVG@transformcm{1.0}{0.0}{0.0}{1.0}{9.24095pt}{0.0pt}\lxSVG@begingroup@{transform=matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 12.79 0)} \lxSVG@begingroup@{_scopebegin=1} \lxSVG@begingroup@{stroke-dasharray=none,stroke-dashoffset=0.0pt} \lxSVG@begingroup@{stroke-linecap=round} \lxSVG@begingroup@{stroke-linejoin=round} \lxSVG@drawpath@unclipped{M -2.55 2.82 C -2.08 1.13 -1.04 0.33 0 0 C -1.04 -0.33 -2.08 -1.13 -2.55 -2.82}{fill:none} \lxSVG@closescope \lxSVG@closescope }}{{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}}{{{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}}{\lxSVG@begingroup@{_scopebegin=1} \lxSVG@transformcm{1.0}{0.0}{0.0}{1.0}{11.28093pt}{0.0pt}\lxSVG@begingroup@{transform=matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 15.61 0)} \lxSVG@begingroup@{_scopebegin=1} \lxSVG@begingroup@{stroke-dasharray=none,stroke-dashoffset=0.0pt} \lxSVG@begingroup@{stroke-linecap=round} \lxSVG@begingroup@{stroke-linejoin=round} \lxSVG@drawpath@unclipped{M -2.55 2.82 C -2.08 1.13 -1.04 0.33 0 0 C -1.04 -0.33 -2.08 -1.13 -2.55 -2.82}{fill:none} \lxSVG@closescope \lxSVG@closescope }}{{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}}}}\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces \lxSVG@closescope } \lxSVG@closescope {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}\hss}\lxSVG@discardpath\lxSVG@closescope \hss}}\lxSVG@closescope\endpgfpicture}}} , it is also the case of G A ′ G^{\prime}_{A} . This proves that G ′ G^{\prime} admits a two-acyclic factorization system. ∎ Then the underlying undirected graph of G ​ ( L ) ​ [ X ] G(L)[X] , which is triangle-free with a chromatic number equal to 4 4 , does not admit a two-acyclic factorization system by Proposition 3.41 . By Proposition 3.42 , any multigraph obtained from the underlying undirected graph of G ​ ( L ) ​ [ X ] G(L)[X] by adding some edges between two elements of G ​ ( L ) ​ [ X ] G(L)[X] already joined by an edge, does not admit either a two-acyclic factorization system. This proves that H ⁡ [ X ] H[X] is not a canonical join graph. 3.5. Dimension of semidistributive extremal lattices In this section we prove Theorem 1.5 from the introduction, that gives a way to compute the dimension of a semidistributive extremal lattice. Definition 3.43 . Let P P be a poset. The critical pairs of P P are the pairs ( a , b ) ∈ P × P (a,b)\in P\times P such that a a and b b are incomparable, for any x < a x<a we have x < b x<b , and for any y > b y>b we have y > a y>a . Definition 3.44 ( [ 48 ] ) . Let P P be a poset. The graph of critical pairs D ⁡ ( P ) D(P) associated to P P is the directed graph whose vertices are the critical pairs and edges are ( a , b ) → ( c , d ) (a,b)\rightarrow(c,d) whenever b ≥ c b\geq c . The critical pairs simplicial complex C ⁡ ( P ) C(P) associated to P P is the simplicial complex whose faces correspond to the subsets of critical pairs that induce an acyclic subgraph of D ⁡ ( P ) D(P) (we mean that we need to avoid directed cycles). A cover set of a simplicial complex Δ \Delta is a subset S S of the faces of Δ \Delta such that every vertex of Δ \Delta is contained in a face of S S . The next result follows from [ 59 , Chapter 4, Proposition 2.1] , and we restate it as it is done in [ 48 , Proposition 14 and the discussion that follows] . Theorem 3.45 . For any poset P P , the dimension of P P is equal to the minimum size of a cover set of C ⁡ ( P ) C(P) . The following is easy to prove. Lemma 3.46 . Let L L be a semidistributive lattice. Then the critical pairs are exactly the pairs ( j , κ ⁡ ( j ) ) (j,\kappa(j)) for j ∈ JIrr ⁡ ( L ) j\in\mathrm{JIrr}(L) with j ∗ ∉ MIrr ⁡ ( L ) j_{*}\not\in\mathrm{MIrr}(L) , which means κ ⁡ ( j ) ≠ j ∗ \kappa(j)\neq j_{*} . Thus, for L L a semidistributive lattice, we identify a critical pair with the join-irreducible element given by its first coordinate. Then the graph D ⁡ ( L ) D(L) is the graph on vertices the join-irreducible elements j j such that κ ⁡ ( j ) ≠ j ∗ \kappa(j)\neq j_{*} , with an edge i → j i\rightarrow j whenever κ ⁡ ( i ) ≥ j \kappa(i)\geq j . Looking at Proposition 2.9 , when L L is a semidistributive extremal lattice, we see that D ⁡ ( L ) D(L) and the Galois graph G ⁡ ( L ) G(L) are similar. We make this observation precise in the next result. a a b b κ ⁡ ( c ) = g \kappa(c)=g c c d d e = κ ⁡ ( a ) e=\kappa(a) κ ⁡ ( b ) \kappa(b) κ ⁡ ( e ) \kappa(e) κ ⁡ ( f ) \kappa(f) f f κ ⁡ ( d ) \kappa(d) κ ⁡ ( g ) \kappa(g) (a) A semidistributive extremal lattice L L g g f f e e d d c c b b a a (b) In black the Galois graph G ⁡ ( L ) G(L) of the lattice from Figure 6(a) . With the extra red 2-cycles it is D ⁡ ( L ) D(L) . Figure 6 . A 2-cycle on two vertices x x and y y in a directed graph will be represented by x ⟷ y x\longleftrightarrow y . See Figure 6 , which is partly taken from [ 47 , Figures 1 and 2] , for an illustration of the following result in a case where there are no j j such that κ ⁡ ( j ) = j ∗ \kappa(j)=j_{*} . ∙ \bullet ∙ \bullet ∙ \bullet ∙ \bullet ∙ \bullet ∙ \bullet ∙ \bullet ∙ \bullet ∙ \bullet Figure 7 . An extremal lattice L L that is not semidistributive. Lemma 3.47 . Let L L be a semidistributive extremal lattice. The graph of critical pairs D ⁡ ( L ) D(L) is obtained from the Galois graph G ⁡ ( L ) G(L) by adding a 2 2 -cycle between all pairs of join-irreducible elements i i and j j that form an independent set of G ⁡ ( L ) G(L) , and then removing the vertices j j such that κ ⁡ ( j ) = j ∗ \kappa(j)=j_{*} . Proof. Let us prove that if i , j ∈ JIrr ⁡ ( L ) i,j\in\mathrm{JIrr}(L) such that κ ⁡ ( i ) ≠ i ∗ \kappa(i)\neq i_{*} and κ ⁡ ( j ) ≠ j ∗ \kappa(j)\neq j_{*} , then we have i → j i\rightarrow j in D ⁡ ( L ) D(L) if and only if j → i j\rightarrow i is not in G ⁡ ( L ) G(L) . This follows from the definitions of the arrows in each graph; i → j i\rightarrow j is in D ⁡ ( L ) D(L) means κ ⁡ ( i ) ≥ j \kappa(i)\geq j , whereas by Proposition 2.9 , j → i j\rightarrow i is not in G ⁡ ( L ) G(L) means j ≤ κ ⁡ ( i ) j\leq\kappa(i) . Thus if we have 2 2 -cycles in G ⁡ ( L ) G(L) they disappear in D ⁡ ( L ) D(L) , but since G ⁡ ( L ) G(L) is acyclic by Proposition 2.4 , G ⁡ ( L ) G(L) does not have 2 2 -cycles. The Lemma follows. ∎ Lemma 3.48 . Let L L be a semidistributive extremal lattice. The isolated vertices of G ⁡ ( L ) ¯ \overline{G(L)} are the join-irreducible elements i ∈ JIrr ⁡ ( L ) i\in\mathrm{JIrr}(L) such that κ ⁡ ( i ) = i ∗ \kappa(i)=i_{*} . Proof. Suppose that i ∈ JIrr ⁡ ( L ) i\in\mathrm{JIrr}(L) such that κ ⁡ ( i ) = i ∗ \kappa(i)=i_{*} . Let us prove that i i is an isolated vertex of G ⁡ ( L ) ¯ \overline{G(L)} , which means that for every j ∈ JIrr ⁡ ( L ) j\in\mathrm{JIrr}(L) , { i , j } \{i,j\} is not an independent set. Seeking a contradiction, assume that { i , j } \{i,j\} is an independent set of G ⁡ ( L ) G(L) , which means i ≤ κ ⁡ ( j ) i\leq\kappa(j) and j ≤ κ ⁡ ( i ) = i ∗ j\leq\kappa(i)=i_{*} . This gives j < κ ⁡ ( j ) j<\kappa(j) , which is absurd. Suppose that i ∈ JIrr ⁡ ( L ) i\in\mathrm{JIrr}(L) such that κ ⁡ ( i ) ≠ i ∗ \kappa(i)\neq i_{*} . We want to prove that i i is not an isolated vertex of G ⁡ ( L ) ¯ \overline{G(L)} . Since L L is semidistributive and extremal, by Lemma 2.11 the downward label sets of L L are the independence sets of its Galois graph G ⁡ ( L ) G(L) . In a semidistributive lattice, the set of all downward label sets is the same as that of all upward label sets, which are denoted by U ⁡ ( x ) U(x) for an element x ∈ L x\in L [ 55 , Corollary 6.4] . By definition of γ J \gamma_{J} , we have i ∈ U ⁡ ( i ∗ ) i\in U(i_{*}) . Since κ ⁡ ( i ) ≠ i ∗ \kappa(i)\neq i_{*} , we have | U ⁡ ( i ∗ ) | ≥ 2 |U(i_{*})|\geq 2 . Thus i i is part of an independence set of size bigger than two, which proves that it is not an isolated vertex of G ⁡ ( L ) ¯ \overline{G(L)} . ∎ For L L a semidistributive extremal lattice, we denote by K ⁡ ( L ) K(L) the undirected graph formed by the subgraph of D ⁡ ( L ) D(L) obtained by keeping only the 2 2 -cycles x ⟷ y x\longleftrightarrow y of D ⁡ ( L ) D(L) and replacing these by an edge joining x x and y y . The null graph is the graph that does not have vertices. An empty graph is a graph without edges (but it can have vertices). Proposition 3.49 . Let L L be a semidistributive extremal lattice. Then G ⁡ ( L ) ¯ \overline{G(L)} is the disjoint union of the graph K ⁡ ( L ) K(L) with the empty graph whose vertices are the i ∈ JIrr ⁡ ( L ) i\in\mathrm{JIrr}(L) such that κ ⁡ ( i ) = i ∗ \kappa(i)=i_{*} . Proof. By Lemma 3.48 , we know that the isolated vertices of G ⁡ ( L ) ¯ \overline{G(L)} are the join-irreducible elements i ∈ JIrr ⁡ ( L ) i\in\mathrm{JIrr}(L) such that κ ⁡ ( i ) = i ∗ \kappa(i)=i_{*} . Then the proposition follows from Lemma 3.47 . ∎ Proposition 3.50 . Let L L be a semidistributive extremal lattice. The cover sets of C ⁡ ( L ) C(L) are the partitions of the vertices of K ⁡ ( L ) K(L) into independent sets. Proof. By definition the vertices of C ⁡ ( L ) C(L) and K ⁡ ( L ) K(L) are the same. Let S S be a cover set of C ⁡ ( L ) C(L) . We know that S S is a partition of the vertices of K ⁡ ( L ) K(L) , and we want to prove that it is a partition into independent sets. Let F ∈ S F\in S . By hypothesis F F is a face of C ⁡ ( L ) C(L) , which means that the induced subgraph of D ⁡ ( L ) D(L) on F F does not have a directed cycle. In particular it does not contain 2 2 -cycles, which proves that F F is an independent set of K ⁡ ( L ) K(L) . Conversely, let S S be a partition of K ⁡ ( L ) K(L) into independent sets. We know that it is a partition of the vertices of C ⁡ ( L ) C(L) . Let F ∈ S F\in S . By hypothesis F F does not contain 2 2 -cycles. But by Lemma 3.47 and the fact that G ⁡ ( L ) G(L) does not have a directed cycle (Proposition 2.4 ), it follows that the only directed cycles in D ⁡ ( L ) D(L) are 2 2 -cycles. Thus the induced subgraph of D ⁡ ( L ) D(L) on F F does not contain directed cycles, which proves that F F is a face of C ⁡ ( L ) C(L) . Thus S S is a cover set of C ⁡ ( L ) C(L) . ∎ We can now prove the main result of this section (which is Theorem 1.5 from the introduction): Theorem 3.51 . Let L L be a semidistributive extremal lattice with | L | > 1 |L|>1 , then dim ( L ) = χ ⁡ ( G ⁡ ( L ) ¯ ) \dim(L)~=~\chi(\overline{G(L)}) . Proof. If L L is a chain with at least two elements, then the underlying undirected graph of G ⁡ ( L ) G(L) is the complete graph on | L | − 1 |L|-1 vertices. Thus G ⁡ ( L ) ¯ \overline{G(L)} is an empty graph with at least one vertex, and the result follows. If L L is not a chain, then the underlying undirected graph of G ⁡ ( L ) G(L) is not the complete graph. Thus, by Proposition 3.49 , K ⁡ ( L ) K(L) is not the null graph and χ ⁡ ( K ⁡ ( L ) ) = χ ⁡ ( G ⁡ ( L ) ¯ ) \chi(K(L))=\chi(\overline{G(L)}) . Then we conclude using Theorem 3.45 and Proposition 3.50 which together give χ ⁡ ( K ⁡ ( L ) ) = dim ( L ) \chi(K(L))=\dim(L) . ∎ Remark 3.52 . We defined the Galois graph for any extremal lattice L L , without the assumption that L L is semidistributive. The lattice L L from Figure 7 shows that it is necessary to have the semidistributivity in Theorem 3.51 . Indeed, L L is extremal but not semidistributive, dim ( L ) = 3 \dim(L)=3 and the complement of its Galois graph is a path on 4 4 vertices, thus χ ⁡ ( G ⁡ ( L ) ¯ ) = 2 \chi(\overline{G(L)})=2 . Corollary 3.53 ( [ 21 ] ) . Let L L be a distributive lattice. Then dim ( L ) = width ⁡ ( JIrr ⁡ ( L ) ) \dim(L)=\mathrm{width}(\mathrm{JIrr}(L)) . Proof. By the fundamental theorem of finite distributive lattices, the Galois graph G ⁡ ( L ) G(L) is isomorphic to the Hasse diagram of the subposet JIrr ⁡ ( L ) \mathrm{JIrr}(L) . Indeed, we have an edge i → j i\rightarrow j in G ⁡ ( L ) G(L) if and only if i ≥ j i\geq j in the subposet JIrr ⁡ ( L ) \mathrm{JIrr}(L) . Thus G ⁡ ( L ) ¯ \overline{G(L)} is isomorphic to the incomparability graph of JIrr ⁡ ( L ) \mathrm{JIrr}(L) . By Dilworth’s decomposition theorem, the chromatic number of this latter graph equals the maximum size of an antichain, which is width ⁡ ( JIrr ⁡ ( L ) ) \mathrm{width}(\mathrm{JIrr}(L)) . We conclude using Theorem 3.51 . ∎ For a finite simple graph G G , we denote by ω ⁡ ( G ) \omega(G) its clique number and by d ⁡ ( G ) d(G) its maximum degree. The following is well-known and easy: Lemma 3.54 . For any simple graph G G , we have ω ⁡ ( G ) ≤ χ ⁡ ( G ) ≤ d ⁡ ( G ) + 1 \omega(G)\leq\chi(G)\leq d(G)+1 . The number of independent sets of G ⁡ ( L ) G(L) is the number of elements of L L ( [ 55 , Corollary 5.6] ). This corresponds to the number of cliques of G ⁡ ( L ) ¯ \overline{G(L)} . By Lemma 2.11 , a clique of G ⁡ ( L ) ¯ \overline{G(L)} corresponds to the downward label set, or upward label set using [ 55 , Corollary 6.4] , of some element of L L . Thus ω ⁡ ( G ⁡ ( L ) ¯ ) \omega(\overline{G(L)}) is the maximum number of elements that cover, or are covered, by some vertex of L L . This is a lower bound for the dimension of L L by Theorem 3.51 and Lemma 3.54 . We obtained differently this result in Proposition 2.20 , proving it is true for any semidistributive lattice. 4. Applications of our dimension result In this section, we use Theorem 3.51 to obtain the dimension of some semidistributive extremal lattices that appeared recently in algebraic combinatorics. 4.1. Hochschild and bubble lattices In this subsection we determine the dimension of the Hochschild lattice [ 16 , 41 ] and of one of its generalizations called the bubble lattices [ 36 , 37 ] . The Hochschild lattice is obtained as an orientation of the 1-skeleton of the Hochschild polytopes, which appeared in algebraic topology [ 52 ] . Recall the discussion on extremal lattices from Section 2.2 , in particular Theorem 2.5 , which enables us to define an extremal lattice by giving its Galois graph. ( 2 , 3 ) (2,3) ( 1 , 3 ) (1,3) ( 1 , 2 ) (1,2) ( 1 , 1 ) (1,1) ( 2 , 2 ) (2,2) (a) The Galois graph G ⁡ ( Hoch ⁡ ( 3 ) ) G(\mathrm{Hoch}(3)) . 220 220 030 030 100 100 110 110 200 200 222 222 111 111 003 003 (b) The Galois graph G ⁡ ( W ⁡ ( 2 , 3 ) ) G(W(2,3)) . Figure 8 . Two Galois graphs. ( − , 11 ​ | 12 | ​ 13 ​ | 22 | ​ 23 ) \Bigl(-,11|12|13|22|23\Bigr) ( 11 , 12 | 13 ​ | 22 | ​ 23 ) \Bigl(11,12|13|22|23\Bigr) ( 11 | 12 , 13 ​ | 22 | ​ 23 ) \Bigl(11|12,13|22|23\Bigr) ( 22 , 11 ​ | 13 | ​ 23 ) \Bigl(22,11|13|23\Bigr) ( 23 , 11 ​ | 12 | ​ 22 ) \Bigl(23,11|12|22\Bigr) ( 11 ​ | 12 | ​ 22 , 13 | 23 ) \Bigl(11|12|22,13|23\Bigr) ( 11 ​ | 12 | ​ 13 , 22 | 23 ) \Bigl(11|12|13,22|23\Bigr) ( 11 | 23 , 12 | 22 ) \Bigl(11|23,12|22\Bigr) ( 11 ​ | 12 | ​ 13 | 22 , 23 ) \Bigl(11|12|13|22,23\Bigr) ( 11 ​ | 12 | ​ 13 | 23 , 22 ) \Bigl(11|12|13|23,22\Bigr) ( 22 | 23 , 11 ) \Bigl(22|23,11\Bigr) ( 11 ​ | 12 | ​ 13 ​ | 22 | ​ 23 , − ) \Bigl(11|12|13|22|23,-\Bigr) Figure 9 . The lattice of maximal orthogonal pairs of G ⁡ ( Hoch ⁡ ( 3 ) ) G(\mathrm{Hoch}(3)) from Figure 8(a) , where pairs ( i , j ) (i,j) are abbreviated by the words i ​ j ij and we omitted set brackets and replaced commas by vertical bars. Figure 10 . The lattice W ⁡ ( 2 , 3 ) W(2,3) . Definition 4.1 ( [ 41 , Theorem 1.1] ) . For any n > 0 n>0 , the Hochschild lattice Hoch ⁡ ( n ) \mathrm{Hoch}(n) is an extremal lattice whose Galois graph is isomorphic to the directed graph whose vertices are { ( i , j ) ∣ i ∈ { 1 , 2 } , i ≤ j ≤ n } \{(i,j)\mid i\in\{1,2\},\,i\leq j\leq n\} and the edges are ( i , j ) → ( i ′ , j ′ ) (i,j)\rightarrow(i^{\prime},j^{\prime}) if ( i , j ) ≠ ( i ′ , j ′ ) (i,j)\neq(i^{\prime},j^{\prime}) and either we have i = 2 i=2 , i ′ = 1 i^{\prime}=1 and j = j ′ j=j^{\prime} , or we have i = i ′ = 1 i=i^{\prime}=1 and j > j ′ j>j^{\prime} . We give in Figure 8(a) the Galois graph of Hoch ⁡ ( 3 ) \mathrm{Hoch}(3) , and we give this lattice in Figure 9 (this figure is taken from [ 41 ] ). Definition 4.2 ( [ 36 , Proposition 4.27] ) . Let X = { x 1 , … , x m } X=\{x_{1},\dots,x_{m}\} and Y = { y 1 , … , y n } Y=\{y_{1},\dots,y_{n}\} be two disjoint linearly ordered alphabets. For any m , n ≥ 0 m,n\geq 0 , the bubble lattice Bub ⁡ ( m , n ) \mathrm{Bub}(m,n) is an extremal lattice whose Galois graph is the directed graph whose vertices are X ⊔ Y ⊔ ( X × Y ) X\sqcup Y\sqcup(X\times Y) and the edges are l 1 → l 2 l_{1}\rightarrow l_{2} if l 1 ≠ l 2 l_{1}\neq l_{2} and either: ∙ \bullet l 1 = ( x s , y t ) l_{1}=(x_{s},y_{t}) and l 2 = x s l_{2}=x_{s} , or ∙ \bullet l 1 = y t l_{1}=y_{t} and l 2 = ( x s , y t ) l_{2}=(x_{s},y_{t}) , or ∙ \bullet l 1 = ( x s , y t ) l_{1}=(x_{s},y_{t}) and l 2 = ( x s ′ , y t ′ ) l_{2}=(x_{s^{\prime}},y_{t^{\prime}}) with s ≥ s ′ s\geq s^{\prime} and t ≤ t ′ t\leq t^{\prime} . It is proved in [ 36 , Proposition 4.19] that Bub ⁡ ( n − 1 , 1 ) \mathrm{Bub}(n-1,1) is isomorphic to Hoch ⁡ ( n ) \mathrm{Hoch}(n) . Moreover, these lattices are semidistributive and extremal [ 36 , Theorem 1.1] . Now we use Theorem 3.51 to obtain the dimension of Hoch ⁡ ( n ) \mathrm{Hoch}(n) . Proposition 4.3 . We have dim ( Hoch ⁡ ( n ) ) = n \dim(\mathrm{Hoch}(n))=n for all n > 0 n>0 . Proof. From the Galois graph of L := Hoch ⁡ ( n ) L:=\mathrm{Hoch}(n) given in Definition 4.1 , we can easily deduce G ⁡ ( L ) ¯ \overline{G(L)} . Let A := { ( 1 , i ) ∣ i ∈ [ n ] } A:=\{(1,i)\mid i\in[n]\} and B := { ( 2 , i ) ∣ 2 ≤ i ≤ n } B:=\{(2,i)\mid 2\leq i\leq n\} . Then G ⁡ ( L ) ¯ \overline{G(L)} is the graph whose vertices are A ⊔ B A\sqcup B , and it can be described by the fact that the induced subgraph on A A is an empty graph, and for every i ≥ 2 i\geq 2 the induced subgraph on { ( 1 , i ) } ⊔ B \{(1,i)\}\sqcup B is the complete graph on n n vertices. From the latter we obtain χ ⁡ ( G ⁡ ( L ) ¯ ) ≥ n \chi(\overline{G(L)})\geq n . Moreover, by coloring the vertices of { ( 1 , 1 ) } ⊔ B \{(1,1)\}\sqcup B with n n different colors, and coloring the vertices of A A all with the same color as ( 1 , 1 ) (1,1) , this gives a proper coloring with n n colors. Thus χ ⁡ ( G ⁡ ( L ) ¯ ) = n \chi(\overline{G(L)})=n , which implies the proposition, by Theorem 3.51 . ∎ In fact, we can directly obtain the dimension of the bubble lattices (which is [ 36 , Conjecture 4.24] ) using the bounds on dimension from Proposition 2.19 , which also implies Proposition 4.3 . Proposition 4.4 . We have dim ( Bub ⁡ ( m , n ) ) = m + n \dim(\mathrm{Bub}(m,n))=m+n for all m , n ≥ 0 m,n\geq 0 . Proof. In [ 36 , Corollary 4.10] , it is proved that the subposet JIrr ⁡ ( Bub ⁡ ( m , n ) ) \mathrm{JIrr}(\mathrm{Bub}(m,n)) on the join-irreducible elements of Bub ⁡ ( m , n ) \mathrm{Bub}(m,n) is the disjoint union of an m m -element antichain with n n chains. This proves that Bub ⁡ ( m , n ) \mathrm{Bub}(m,n) has m + n m+n atoms and width ⁡ ( JIrr ⁡ ( Bub ⁡ ( m , n ) ) ) = m + n \mathrm{width}\big(\mathrm{JIrr}(\mathrm{Bub}(m,n))\big)=m+n , which is an upper-bound of dim ( Bub ⁡ ( m , n ) ) \dim(\mathrm{Bub}(m,n)) by Proposition 2.19 . Since Bub ⁡ ( m , n ) \mathrm{Bub}(m,n) is a semidistributive lattice with m + n m+n atoms, using Proposition 2.20 we have dim ( Bub ⁡ ( m , n ) ) ≥ m + n \dim(\mathrm{Bub}(m,n))\geq m+n . This implies the proposition. ∎ 4.2. ( m , n ) (m,n) -word lattices We focus in this section on another generalization of the Hochschild lattice, called ( m , n ) (m,n) -word lattices ( [ 45 , Definition 77] , [ 43 ] ). Definition 4.5 ( [ 45 , Definition 77] ) . Let m , n ≥ 0 m,n\geq 0 . The ( m , n ) (m,n) -words are the words w = w 1 ⋯ w n w=w_{1}\cdots w_{n} of length n n on the alphabet { 0 , 1 , … , m + 1 } \{0,1,\dots,m+1\} such that w 1 ≠ m + 1 w_{1}\neq m+1 and for s ∈ [ m ] s\in[m] , w i = s w_{i}=s implies w j ≥ s w_{j}\geq s for all j < i j<i . The ( m , n ) (m,n) -word poset W ⁡ ( m , n ) W(m,n) is the poset on ( m , n ) (m,n) -words with the product order, which means w ≤ w ′ w\leq w^{\prime} if w i ≤ w i ′ w_{i}\leq w^{\prime}_{i} for all i ∈ [ n ] i\in[n] . The poset W ⁡ ( m , n ) W(m,n) is a lattice [ 45 , Corollary 84] and is semidistributive and extremal [ 43 , Theorem 1.1] . Similarly to the bubble lattice Bub ⁡ ( m , n ) \mathrm{Bub}(m,n) , it is isomorphic to Hoch ⁡ ( n ) \mathrm{Hoch}(n) when m = 1 m=1 [ 45 , Example 78] . But these are different generalizations of the Hochschild lattice. See Figure 8(b) for the Galois graph of W ⁡ ( 2 , 3 ) W(2,3) , and Figure 10 for the lattice W ⁡ ( 2 , 3 ) W(2,3) . Lemma 4.6 ( [ 43 , Lemma 12] ) . The join-irreducible elements of W ⁡ ( m , n ) W(m,n) are the ( m , n ) (m,n) -words a ( i , j ) a^{(i,j)} for i ∈ [ n ] i\in[n] and j ∈ [ m ] j\in[m] , and b ( i ) b^{(i)} for i ∈ { 2 , … , n } i\in\{2,\dots,n\} , where a ( i , j ) a^{(i,j)} is the ( m , n ) (m,n) -word with the first i i letters equal to j j and other letters are 0 0 , and b ( i ) b^{(i)} is the ( m , n ) (m,n) -word whose i i -th letter is m + 1 m+1 and all other letters are 0 0 . Proposition 4.7 ( [ 43 , Proposition 22] ) . The downward label set of w ∈ W ⁡ ( m , n ) w\in W(m,n) is { a ( i , j ) ∣ i is the position of the rightmost letter j in w , where j ∈ [ m ] } ⊔ { b ( i ) ∣ w i = m + 1 } \{a^{(i,j)}\mid\text{$i$ is the position of the rightmost letter $j$ in $w$, where $j\in[m]$}\}\sqcup\{b^{(i)}\mid w_{i}=m+1\} . Proposition 4.8 . We have dim ( W ⁡ ( m , n ) ) = n \dim(W(m,n))=n for all m , n ≥ 0 m,n\geq 0 . Proof. Let m , n ≥ 0 m,n\geq 0 and let L := W ⁡ ( m , n ) L:=W(m,n) . By Theorem 3.51 , we have dim ( L ) = χ ⁡ ( G ⁡ ( L ) ¯ ) \dim(L)=\chi(\overline{G(L)}) , which is the chromatic number of the canonical join graph of L L . Color a join-irreducible element of L L by the position of its rightmost non-zero letter. By Proposition 4.7 , this is a proper coloring with n n colors of G ⁡ ( L ) ¯ \overline{G(L)} , thus dim ( L ) ≤ n \dim(L)\leq n . As the downward label set of the maximum element m ⁡ ( m + 1 ) ​ … ​ ( m + 1 ) m(m+1)\dots(m+1) of L L has n n elements, thus χ ⁡ ( G ⁡ ( L ) ¯ ) ≥ n \chi(\overline{G(L)})\geq n , and the proposition follows. ∎ Remark 4.9 . We saw that the bounds from Proposition 2.19 were enough to find the dimension of the bubble lattices. For W ⁡ ( m , n ) W(m,n) it is not enough as [ 43 , Lemma 24] gives its subposet on its join-irreducible elements and it follows that width ⁡ ( JIrr ⁡ ( W ⁡ ( m , n ) ) ) = n + 1 \mathrm{width}\big(\mathrm{JIrr}(W(m,n))\big)=n+1 , which is strictly bigger than dim ( W ⁡ ( m , n ) ) = n \dim(W(m,n))=n . 4.3. Parabolic Tamari lattices In this section, we are interested in the parabolic Tamari lattice [ 39 , 40 ] , which is a generalization of the Tamari lattice. Figure 11 . A noncrossing α \alpha -partition. (a) The Galois graph G ⁡ ( Tam ⁡ ( 1 , 2 , 1 ) ) G(\mathrm{Tam}(1,2,1)) . ∙ \bullet ∙ \bullet ∙ \bullet ∙ \bullet ∙ \bullet ∙ \bullet ∙ \bullet ∙ \bullet ∙ \bullet ∙ \bullet (b) The lattice Tam ⁡ ( 1 , 2 , 1 ) \mathrm{Tam}(1,2,1) . Figure 12 . The parabolic Tamari lattice Tam ⁡ ( 1 , 2 , 1 ) \mathrm{Tam}(1,2,1) . Let α = ( α 1 , … , α k ) \alpha=(\alpha_{1},\dots,\alpha_{k}) be an integer composition of n > 0 n>0 . We draw n n horizontally aligned nodes labeled 1 , … , n 1,\dots,n and put vertical bars separating the node α 1 + ⋯ + α i \alpha_{1}+\cdots+\alpha_{i} from the node α 1 + ⋯ + α i + 1 \alpha_{1}+\cdots+\alpha_{i}+1 for all i ∈ [ k ] i\in[k] . The sets of nodes between two such separating bars are the α \alpha -regions, they are naturally labeled 1 , … , k 1,\dots,k from left to right. An α \alpha -arc is a pair ( a , b ) (a,b) , where 1 ≤ a < b ≤ n 1\leq a<b\leq n and a , b a,b are in different α \alpha -regions. We represent graphically an α \alpha -arc ( a , b ) (a,b) by drawing a curve leaving the bottom of node a a , staying below the α \alpha -region containing a a , moving up and above the following α \alpha -regions until it reaches the top of node b b . We will also denote an α \alpha -arc ( a , b ) (a,b) by w a , b w_{a,b} . Two α \alpha -arcs ( a 1 , b 1 ) (a_{1},b_{1}) and ( a 2 , b 2 ) (a_{2},b_{2}) are compatible if a 1 ≠ a 2 a_{1}\neq a_{2} , b 1 ≠ b 2 b_{1}\neq b_{2} , and if a 1 < a 2 < b 1 < b 2 a_{1}<a_{2}<b_{1}<b_{2} then either a 1 , a 2 a_{1},a_{2} or a 2 , b 1 a_{2},b_{1} are in the same α \alpha -region, and if a 1 < a 2 < b 2 < b 1 a_{1}<a_{2}<b_{2}<b_{1} then a 1 , a 2 a_{1},a_{2} are in the same α \alpha -region. A set of pairwise compatible α \alpha -arcs are called noncrossing α \alpha -partitions . The set of all noncrossing α \alpha -partitions is denoted by Nonc ⁡ ( α ) \mathrm{Nonc}(\alpha) . See Figure 11 (which is taken from [ 40 ] ) for an illustration of a noncrossing α \alpha -partition, where α \alpha -regions are depicted with different colors. In this figure α = ( 3 , 4 , 2 , 1 , 4 , 2 ) \alpha=(3,4,2,1,4,2) and the two red α \alpha -arcs are ( 1 , 5 ) (1,5) and ( 5 , 13 ) (5,13) . Definition 4.10 ( [ 40 , Theorem 1.3] ) . Let α \alpha be an integer composition of n > 0 n>0 . The parabolic Tamari lattice Tam ⁡ ( α ) \mathrm{Tam}(\alpha) is an extremal lattice whose Galois graph is isomorphic to the directed graph whose vertices are the α \alpha -arcs w a , b w_{a,b} and the edges are between different α \alpha -arcs w a 1 , b 1 → w a 2 , b 2 w_{a_{1},b_{1}}\rightarrow w_{a_{2},b_{2}} such that either a 1 a_{1} and a 2 a_{2} are in the same α \alpha -region and a 1 ≤ a 2 < b 2 ≤ b 1 a_{1}\leq a_{2}<b_{2}\leq b_{1} , or a 1 a_{1} and a 2 a_{2} are in different α \alpha -regions and a 2 < a 1 < b 2 ≤ b 1 a_{2}<a_{1}<b_{2}\leq b_{1} , where a 1 a_{1} and b 2 b_{2} are also in different α \alpha -regions. See Figure 12 for an example. This lattice was defined in [ 39 ] . The fact that they are semidistributive and extremal is [ 40 , Theorem 1.1] . If α = ( 1 , 1 , … , 1 ) \alpha=(1,1,\dots,1) then Tam ⁡ ( α ) \mathrm{Tam}(\alpha) is the Tamari lattice [ 39 , Proposition 8] . Using [ 40 , Proposition 3.5] , it follows that the canonical join graph of Tam ⁡ ( α ) \mathrm{Tam}(\alpha) , which is G ⁡ ( Tam ⁡ ( α ) ) ¯ \overline{G(\mathrm{Tam}(\alpha))} , is the graph whose vertices are the α \alpha -arcs, with an edge between two compatible such arcs. Thus its cliques are the noncrossing α \alpha -partitions. Proposition 4.11 . Let α = ( α 1 , … , α k ) \alpha=(\alpha_{1},\dots,\alpha_{k}) be an integer composition of n > 0 n>0 . Denote by max ⁡ ( α ) \max(\alpha) the biggest integer appearing in α \alpha . We have dim ( Tam ⁡ ( α ) ) = n − max ⁡ ( α ) \dim(\mathrm{Tam}(\alpha))=n-\max(\alpha) . Proof. Let i i such that α i = max ⁡ ( α ) \alpha_{i}=\max(\alpha) . A proper coloring of G ⁡ ( Tam ⁡ ( α ) ) ¯ \overline{G(\mathrm{Tam}(\alpha))} with n − max ⁡ ( α ) n-\max(\alpha) colors can be obtained by coloring an α \alpha -arc ( a , b ) (a,b) by a a if a a is in an α \alpha -region labeled by k < i k<i , and the other α \alpha -arcs ( a ′ , b ′ ) (a^{\prime},b^{\prime}) which have not yet been assigned a color are colored by b ′ b^{\prime} . Thus the colors are labels of nodes that are not in the i i -th α \alpha -region, thus this uses at most (in fact exactly) n − max ⁡ ( α ) n-\max(\alpha) colors. This is a proper coloring since two α \alpha -arcs having the same color share the same starting node, or ending node, thus they are not compatible. This proves that χ ⁡ ( G ⁡ ( Tam ⁡ ( α ) ) ¯ ) ≤ n − max ⁡ ( α ) \chi(\overline{G(\mathrm{Tam}(\alpha))})\leq n-\max(\alpha) . We still need to prove that χ ⁡ ( G ⁡ ( Tam ⁡ ( α ) ) ¯ ) ≥ n − max ⁡ ( α ) \chi(\overline{G(\mathrm{Tam}(\alpha))})\geq n-\max(\alpha) . This comes from the fact that we can find a noncrossing α \alpha -partition with this number of α \alpha -arcs; for every r ∈ [ k ] r\in[k] draw an α \alpha -arc between the j t ​ h j^{th} node of the r r -th α \alpha -region and the j t ​ h j^{th} node of the ( r + 1 ) (r+1) -st α \alpha -region whenever these nodes exist. This forms a noncrossing α \alpha -partition with n − max ⁡ ( α ) n-\max(\alpha) total number of α \alpha -arcs. ∎ For example, the planar lattice Tam ⁡ ( 1 , 2 , 1 ) \mathrm{Tam}(1,2,1) represented in Figure 12 is of dimension 2 2 . 4.4. On some lattices of torsion classes In this section we will mainly focus on the lattices of torsion classes of a gentle tree, but we also include a short discussion about the lattices of torsion classes of the simply laced Dynkin quivers 𝐀 \mathbf{A} , 𝐃 \mathbf{D} and 𝐄 \mathbf{E} . Proposition 4.12 ( [ 55 , Theorem 2.10] ) . If A A is representation finite and mod ​ A \mathrm{mod}\,A has no cycles, then Tors ⁡ ( A ) \mathrm{Tors}(A) is a finite semidistributive extremal lattice. This proposition applies to the simply laced Dynkin quivers 𝐀 , 𝐃 , 𝐄 \mathbf{A},\,\mathbf{D},\,\mathbf{E} . Other examples, which contain type 𝐀 \mathbf{A} , are the gentle trees (see Section 2.8 ). Let us first focus on the former. Denote by Camb ⁡ ( Q → ) \mathrm{Camb}(\vec{Q}) the Cambrian lattice associated to an orientation of a simply laced Dynkin diagram Q Q ( [ 50 ] ). It is proved in [ 30 , Section 4.2] that if Q → \vec{Q} is a simply laced Dynkin quiver, then Tors ⁡ ( 𝕂 ​ Q → ) ≅ Camb ⁡ ( Q → ) \mathrm{Tors}(\mathbb{K}\vec{Q})\cong\mathrm{Camb}(\vec{Q}) . For example, it is known that when the path quiver 𝐀 \mathbf{A} is linearly oriented, we obtain the Tamari lattice. See also Figure 14(b) for another example, which is a Cambrian lattice of type 𝐀 3 \mathbf{A}_{3} . If Q → \vec{Q} has n n vertices, then dim ( Camb ⁡ ( Q → ) ) ≥ n \dim(\mathrm{Camb}(\vec{Q}))\geq n using Proposition 2.20 . Also, by definition, Camb ⁡ ( Q → ) \mathrm{Camb}(\vec{Q}) is a subposet of the weak order of the Coxeter group associated to Q Q . In [ 49 ] , it is proved that the dimension of the weak order of type 𝐀 n \mathbf{A}_{n} and 𝐃 n \mathbf{D}_{n} is n n , and bounds are given for the dimension of the weak orders on the exceptional types 𝐄 6 , 𝐄 7 , 𝐄 8 \mathbf{E}_{6},\,\mathbf{E}_{7},\,\mathbf{E}_{8} . It follows that for all orientations of the corresponding Coxeter diagrams, dim ( Camb ⁡ ( 𝐀 n → ) ) = n \dim\big(\mathrm{Camb}(\overrightarrow{\mathbf{A}_{n}})\big)=n and dim ( Camb ⁡ ( 𝐃 n → ) ) = n \dim\big(\mathrm{Camb}(\overrightarrow{\mathbf{D}_{n}})\big)=n . For the exceptional types, with the help of the computer, we obtain: Proposition 4.13 . For all orientations of the corresponding Coxeter diagrams, we have dim ( Camb ⁡ ( 𝐄 6 → ) ) = 6 \dim\big(\mathrm{Camb}(\overrightarrow{\mathbf{E}_{6}})\big)=6 , dim ( Camb ⁡ ( 𝐄 7 → ) ) = 7 \dim\big(\mathrm{Camb}(\overrightarrow{\mathbf{E}_{7}})\big)=7 and dim ( Camb ⁡ ( 𝐄 8 → ) ) = 8 \dim\big(\mathrm{Camb}(\overrightarrow{\mathbf{E}_{8}})\big)=8 . Proof (using a computer). The computations were performed on the Nibi supercomputer, operated by Calcul Québec and the Digital Research Alliance of Canada (Alliance). The Cambrian lattices of any orientation of types 𝐄 6 \mathbf{E}_{6} , 𝐄 7 \mathbf{E}_{7} , and 𝐄 8 \mathbf{E}_{8} have respectively 833 833 , 4160 4160 , and 25080 25080 elements. The number of vertices of their respective Galois graphs are 36 36 , 63 63 and 120 120 . With SageMath, using Theorem 3.51 , we were able to exhaustively check that the Cambrian lattices of the 32 32 different orientations of 𝐄 6 \mathbf{E}_{6} have all dimension 6 6 , the Cambrian lattices of the 64 64 different orientations of 𝐄 7 \mathbf{E}_{7} have all dimension 7 7 , and the Cambrian lattices of the 128 128 different orientations of 𝐄 8 \mathbf{E}_{8} have all dimension 8 8 . The latter required roughly one week of computation. ∎ Remark 4.14 . For the Cambrian lattices of type 𝐀 \mathbf{A} , it is also possible to use a similar combinatorial model to the above discussion about the parabolic Tamari lattice in terms of noncrossing partitions ( [ 51 , Example 4.9] , [ 5 , section 8.3] ). In this model, by coloring an arc with the label of its starting node, we prove analogously to Proposition 4.11 that dim ( Camb ⁡ ( 𝐀 n → ) ) = n \dim\big(\mathrm{Camb}(\overrightarrow{\mathbf{A}_{n}})\big)=n for any orientation of 𝐀 n \mathbf{A}_{n} . Now we focus on the torsion classes of a gentle tree. To see more details about the combinatorics of gentle algebras, see [ 14 , 44 ] . Given a gentle tree T T , there is an (almost) canonical way to draw it so that arrows always go horizontally to the right or vertically upwards, and such that the relations correspond to a change of direction. (It is almost canonical because for a given arrow, we can choose whether to draw it horizontally or vertically; this forces the direction of all other arrows). From now on, we assume that our gentle trees are drawn in this way (having fixed an orientation of the edges). The horizontal arrows can be thought of as east steps and the vertical ones as north steps. Thus, the relations correspond to taking an east step followed by a north step, or a north step followed by an east step. See Figure 14(a) for an example of a gentle tree drawn with our conventions. The indecomposable modules of T T correspond to the walks in the tree which take the unique shortest path in the underlying graph between their two endpoints without containing two steps that form a relation. Those are called the string modules and we write C σ C_{\sigma} for the indecomposable module corresponding to the walk σ \sigma . As a representation this corresponds to putting 𝕂 \mathbb{K} at every vertex visited by σ \sigma , 0 0 at every other vertex and the identity map id between any two adjacent copies of 𝕂 \mathbb{K} . With the orientation of the gentle tree fixed as above, there is a canonical way to read a walk σ \sigma : from top left to bottom right. Each walk therefore has a well-defined top left endpoint, and a well-defined bottom right endpoint. We refer to them simply as the left endpoint and the right endpoint . Then, any walk σ \sigma can be uniquely written as the word recording the arrows α \alpha or opposite arrows α − 1 \alpha^{-1} met on the way from its left endpoint to its right endpoint. ∙ \bullet ∙ \bullet ∙ \bullet α \alpha β ψ \beta_{\psi} σ ′ \sigma^{\prime} σ \sigma 0 0 𝕂 \mathbb{K} 𝕂 \mathbb{K} 0 0 id 𝕂 \mathbb{K} 𝕂 \mathbb{K} 0 0 id 0 0 0 0 id 0 0 Figure 13 . Illustration of the proof of Lemma 4.15 , showing on the right a part of the non-zero morphism from C σ ′ C_{\sigma^{\prime}} to C σ C_{\sigma} . Lemma 4.15 . Let σ \sigma and σ ′ \sigma^{\prime} be two different walks on a gentle tree T T that share the same right endpoint. Then C σ C_{\sigma} and C σ ′ C_{\sigma^{\prime}} are not hom-orthogonal. Proof. The walks σ , σ ′ \sigma,\sigma^{\prime} , sharing the same right endpoint, can be written σ = σ 1 ​ α ​ γ \sigma=\sigma_{1}\alpha\gamma and σ ′ = σ 1 ′ ​ β − 1 ​ γ \sigma^{\prime}=\sigma^{\prime}_{1}\beta^{-1}\gamma where α ≠ β ψ \alpha\neq\beta_{\psi} . Moreover since the underlying undirected graph of T T is a tree, we know that the walks σ \sigma and σ ′ \sigma^{\prime} have disjoint supports. Without loss of generality, we can assume that α \alpha is an east step and β ψ \beta_{\psi} a north step. Then we have a non-zero morphism from C σ ′ C_{\sigma^{\prime}} to C σ C_{\sigma} , which is the identity map on the vertices of γ \gamma and the zero map on every other vertex. See Figure 13 , with on the right the crucial part of this non-zero morphism. ∎ Proposition 4.16 ( [ 4 , Theorem 1.8] ) . If A A is representation finite, the faces of the canonical join complex are the semibricks. Thus for A A representation finite, G ⁡ ( Tors ⁡ ( A ) ) ¯ \overline{G(\mathrm{Tors}(A))} is the graph whose vertices are the bricks and we have an edge between B B and B ′ B^{\prime} if they are hom-orthogonal. 1 1 3 3 2 2 4 4 b b c c a a (a) A gentle tree T T with relation a ​ b = 0 ab=0 . mod ​ A \mathrm{mod}\,A ∙ \bullet ∙ \bullet ∙ \bullet ∙ \bullet ∙ \bullet ∙ \bullet ∙ \bullet ∙ \bullet ∙ \bullet ∙ \bullet ∙ \bullet ∙ \bullet ∙ \bullet (b) The lattice of torsion classes of 1 → 2 ← 3 1\rightarrow 2\leftarrow 3 . Figure 14 . Proposition 4.17 . Let T T be a gentle tree having n n vertices. Then dim ( Tors ⁡ ( T ) ) = n \dim(\mathrm{Tors}(T))=n . Proof. Since a gentle tree is representation finite, Proposition 4.16 applies. The category mod ​ A \mathrm{mod}\,A has n n simples and they form a semibrick as their supports are disjoint. Thus dim ( Tors ⁡ ( T ) ) ≥ n \dim(\mathrm{Tors}(T))~\geq~n . To conclude, let us prove that dim ( Tors ⁡ ( T ) ) ≤ n \dim(\mathrm{Tors}(T))\leq n . For this we find a proper coloring of G ⁡ ( Tors ⁡ ( T ) ) ¯ \overline{G(\mathrm{Tors}(T))} with n n colors. Since T T is a gentle tree, every brick of T T is a string representation C σ C_{\sigma} , thus it has a right endpoint. Color each brick with the label of its right endpoint. This gives a coloring with n n colors of G ⁡ ( Tors ⁡ ( T ) ) ¯ \overline{G(\mathrm{Tors}(T))} , which is proper by Lemma 4.15 . This proves the proposition. ∎ Corollary 4.18 . We have dim ( Camb ⁡ ( 𝐀 𝐧 → ) ) = n \dim\big(\mathrm{Camb}(\overrightarrow{\mathbf{A_{n}}})\big)=n for any orientation of 𝐀 n \mathbf{A}_{n} . Proof. This follows from Proposition 4.17 since Camb ⁡ ( 𝐀 𝐧 → ) \mathrm{Camb}(\overrightarrow{\mathbf{A_{n}}}) is the lattice of torsion classes of an orientation of the path quiver 𝐀 n \mathbf{A}_{n} , which is a particular gentle tree having n n vertices. ∎ 5. Open questions We finish by giving some open questions. In [ 7 , Figure 9] an example is given of an extremal congruence uniform lattice of dimension 3 obtained from the doubling of a chain, thus of dimension 1, of an extremal congruence uniform lattice of dimension 2. This is a counterexample to the observation that often dim ( L ⁡ [ C ] ) = dim ( L ) \dim(L[C])=\dim(L) when the interval C C satisfies dim ( C ) < dim ( L ) \dim(C)<\dim(L) . Question 5.1 . Let L L be a lattice. Under which condition on an interval C C of L L do we have dim ( L ⁡ [ C ] ) = dim ( L ) \dim(L[C])=\dim(L) ? In Theorem 3.26 , we proved the equivalence between extremality and shellability in the case of congruence uniform lattices. For the more general case of semidistributive lattices, we know that extremality implies shellability (Corollary 3.7 ). What about the converse? Question 5.2 . Is it true that any semidistributive shellable lattice is extremal? Another direction to generalize Theorem 3.26 is to look at join-congruence uniform and meet-congruence uniform lattices. We proved in Corollary 3.25 that any join-congruence uniform lattice that is shellable is join-extremal. What about the converse: Question 5.3 . Is it true that any join-congruence uniform lattice that is join-extremal is shellable? In Section 3.4 we proved that an induced subgraph of a canonical join graph is not always a canonical join graph, even if the initial canonical join graph can be obtained from a semidistributive lattice that is extremal. For similar questions, it would be nice to have an affirmative answer to the following question, as it would restrict the class of lattices we have to look at: Question 5.4 . Let G G be the canonical join graph of a semidistributive lattice. Is it true that G G can also be obtained as the canonical join graph of an extremal semidistributive lattice? Different lattices that we considered in this paper can be obtained as the orientation of the 1-skeleton of a polytope by choosing a generic cost vector (see the precise definition in [ 29 ] ). In our examples, all the ones coming from a n n -dimensional polytope were of order dimension n n . Since in these examples those lattices are all semidistributive we know that we have the lower bound n n for their order dimension by Proposition 2.20 . Question 5.5 . If a lattice L L is obtained as an orientation of the 1-skeleton of a n n -dimensional polytope, is it true that dim ( L ) = n \dim(L)=n ? If it is false, does it hold with the extra hypothesis that L L is semidistributive and/or extremal? Note that it seems that the face lattice associated to a polytope behaves very differently since for any n ≥ 4 n\geq 4 there exist n n -dimensional polytopes whose face lattice has arbitrarily large order dimension, and for n = 3 n=3 the face lattice has order dimension less than or equal to 4 [ 13 ] . The lattices of torsion classes are Hasse-regular lattices, meaning any element x x in their Hasse diagram has the same degree, which is the sum of the number of elements x x covers with the number of elements that cover x x . A lattice is called r r -Hasse-regular if the degree of any element is r r . Question 5.6 . Is it true that any semidistributive r r -Hasse-regular lattice is of dimension r r ? If not, is it true if the lattice is additionally congruence uniform? References [AS25] A. Abram and A. Segovia (2025) Dimension of unicycle posets . arXiv preprint . External Links: 2505.16837 Cited by: §2.7 . [ASS06] I. Assem, D. Simson, and A. Skowroński (2006) Elements of the representation theory of associative algebras: techniques of representation theory . London Mathematical Society Student Texts , Cambridge University Press . Cited by: §2.8 . [BFR72] K. A. Baker, P. C. Fishburn, and F. S. Roberts (1972) Partial orders of dimension 2 . Networks 2 ( 1 ), pp. 11–28 . External Links: Document Cited by: §2.7 . [BCZ19] E. Barnard, A. Carroll, and S. Zhu (2019) Minimal inclusions of torsion classes . Algebraic Combinatorics 2 ( 5 ), pp. 879–901 . Cited by: Proposition 4.16 . [BDH23] E. Barnard, C. Defant, and E. J. Hanson (2023) Pop-stack operators for torsion classes and Cambrian lattices . arXiv preprint . External Links: 2312.03959 Cited by: Remark 4.14 . [BAR17] E. Barnard (2017) The canonical join representation in algebraic combinatorics, Ph.D. 